// English stories. Blocks: ["h", heading] ["p", paragraph] ["t", oral tradition, clearly marked] ["n", note on the record]
window.STORIES = window.STORIES || {};
window.STORIES.en = {

/* ───────────── HEROES ───────────── */

puli: {
  title: "Puli Thevar: the lord who would not pay the rice tribute",
  dek: "Twelve years before Kattabomman was born, a Maravar chieftain in the dry country west of Tirunelveli refused the Nawab's tax and held the Company's armies at bay for more than a decade.",
  body: [
    ["h","A palayam at the foot of the Ghats"],
    ["p","Nerkattumseval sits in the black-soil plain of present-day Tenkasi district, in the old Sankarankoil taluk, with the Western Ghats rising on the horizon. In the eighteenth century it was one of the palayams of the western Tirunelveli country, a military fief held by a Maravar chieftain. Its lord in the middle of the century was Puli Thevar, born, by the date most often given, on 1 September 1715. His father is recorded as Chitraputra Thevan and his wife as Kayalkani Nachiyar."],
    ["p","Puli Thevar inherited a world that was coming apart. The Madurai Nayak kingdom, which had created the palayam system two centuries earlier, had collapsed. The Nawab of Arcot, Mohammed Ali, now claimed the right to tribute from the south, and he had borrowed heavily from the English East India Company to fight his rival Chanda Sahib. To be repaid, the Company would help him squeeze the palayams."],
    ["h","1755: the first expedition turns back"],
    ["p","In March 1755 the Nawab sent his elder brother Mahfuz Khan into Madurai and Tirunelveli with a Company force under Colonel Alexander Heron, some 500 Europeans and 200 sepoys. Madurai fell to them easily. Heron was urged to deal next with Puli Thevar, who was openly defying the Company and who had great influence over the western palayakkarars."],
    ["p","The expedition against Nerkattumseval never succeeded. The Tamil Nadu state history text records that Heron had no cannon, no supplies and no money to pay his soldiers. He abandoned the attack, retreated to Madurai, and was later recalled and dismissed. The first Company army sent to discipline the southern palayams had gone home without its tribute."],
    ["h","A league of the west"],
    ["p","Puli Thevar used the moment to build something the south had not seen: an alliance of palayakkarars against the Nawab and the Company. The palayams of Uthumalai, Surandai, Thalaivankottai, Naduvakurichi, Singampatti, Urkad, Seithur, Kollamkondan and Wadakarai joined him. Of the Maravar palayams only Sivagiri stayed out. The eastern Nayak palayams of Ettayapuram and Panchalankurichi did not join, and the rajas of Ramanathapuram and Pudukkottai sided with the English."],
    ["p","He reached further still. He courted three Pathan officers, Mianah, Mudimiah and Nabikhan Kattak, who held parts of Madurai and Tirunelveli as agents of Chanda Sahib. He sought help from Hyder Ali of Mysore and from the French, though Hyder Ali was then tied down fighting the Marathas. And he won over the Raja of Travancore with a promise to restore Kalakkad to him."],
    ["h","Kalakkad"],
    ["p","Mahfuz Khan returned with 1,000 Company sepoys, 600 more of the Nawab's, and cavalry and infantry from the Carnatic. Before he could post his troops near Kalakkad, 2,000 Travancore soldiers marched in to join Puli Thevar. Mahfuz Khan's army was routed. For the years 1756 to 1763 the state text describes the Tirunelveli palayakkarars under Puli Thevar as being in an almost constant state of rebellion."],
    ["h","The siege of Nerkattumseval"],
    ["p","The Company then sent its most capable Indian commander, Yusuf Khan, born Maruthanayagam Pillai. He would not attack without heavy guns. The siege train from Tiruchirappalli reached him only in September 1760, delayed because the English were fighting the French, Hyder Ali and the Marathas at once. A first attempt on Vasudevanallur failed. Then Yusuf Khan began battering the walls of Nerkattumseval, and the bombardment went on for about two months."],
    ["p","On 16 May 1761 Puli Thevar's three principal forts, Nerkattumseval, Vasudevanallur and Panayur, fell. The wider ground fell away too. With the capture of Pondicherry the French were gone as an ally, and Travancore, Seithur, Uthumalai and Surandai went over to the Company. Puli Thevar slipped away into hiding."],
    ["h","The return, and the silence"],
    ["p","Fortune turned once more. Yusuf Khan quarrelled with his masters, was accused of treachery for dealing with the palayakkarars on his own, and was hanged near Madurai in October 1764. That same year Puli Thevar came back from exile and retook Nerkattumseval. He held it for three more years."],
    ["p","In 1767 the Company sent Captain Campbell, who besieged and captured the fort. Puli Thevar escaped once again. After that the record goes quiet. The state history text says plainly that nothing is definitely known of his last days, and that he died in exile."],
    ["t","Tradition in the Sankarankoil country tells that when he was finally taken, he asked to worship at the Sankaranarayanar temple on the way, and that the soldiers found only his broken chains. Wikipedia gives a death date of 16 October 1767. Neither the escape story nor the date is confirmed by the state record."],
    ["h","Why he matters"],
    ["p","Puli Thevar's war began in 1755, more than a century before the revolt of 1857. He was not fighting for a nation in the modern sense, but for the right of a palayam to refuse an unjust tribute. In doing so he showed every chieftain who came after him that the Company could be resisted, that alliances across palayams and kingdoms were possible, and that a mud and stone fort in the black-soil plain could hold off an army for years."],
    ["n","Main sources: Tamil Nadu state history text, Palayakkarars' Revolt 1755–1801 and Early Resistance of Southern Palayakkarars; Manonmaniam Sundaranar University, History of Tamil Nadu since 1801. The 1752 start date found in some accounts is not used here."]
  ]
},

ondi: {
  title: "Ondiveeran: the commander of Nerkattumseval",
  dek: "Puli Thevar's war was not fought by one man. Among the commanders who led his units into battle, Ondiveeran is the one Tamil memory has kept closest.",
  body: [
    ["h","A soldier of the palayam"],
    ["p","Ondiveeran came from Nerkattumseval itself. The surviving record about him is thin, and most of it comes from the Tamil Nadu state history text, community memory and the citation India Post issued with his stamp in 2022. He belonged to the Pagadai, or Arunthathiyar, community, which honours him as its hero."],
    ["p","The state text describes him as the leader of one of Puli Thevar's army units, and says that fighting beside his chief he caused much damage to the Company's army. Other accounts name a second commander, Venni Kaladi, alongside him. Wikipedia describes him as Puli Thevar's commander-in-chief."],
    ["h","The war years"],
    ["p","Ondiveeran fought through the long war that began with Heron's expedition in 1755: the defence of the western palayams, the clash with Mahfuz Khan's army, and the hard years after Yusuf Khan's siege guns arrived in 1760. The India Post citation lists his units in actions at Gangaikondan, Alwarkurichi, Nerkattumseval, Uthumalai, Surandai and Vasudevanallur between 1757 and 1759, and in the defence of Vasudevanallur against Captain Campbell in 1767."],
    ["t","Oral tradition, recorded in the state history text, tells that in one battle Ondiveeran's hand was cut off, and that Puli Thevar grieved. In the tradition, Ondiveeran answered that the lost hand was a fair price for having entered the enemy's fort and cut down many."],
    ["h","After Puli Thevar"],
    ["p","When Nerkattumseval fell for the last time in 1767 and Puli Thevar vanished, the fighting in the western country did not stop at once. According to the India Post citation, Ondiveeran took on the protection of Puli Thevar's children, his sons Sivagnanapandian and Sithiraputhira Thevar and his daughter Gomathi Muthuthalachi."],
    ["p","He died on 20 August 1771, near Manur in Tirunelveli district, fighting the Company's forces. Wikipedia and the stamp citation agree on the date."],
    ["h","Remembered late"],
    ["p","For two centuries Ondiveeran survived mainly in local memory. In the 2000s his community campaigned, with petitions and protests, for a public memorial. The foundation stone of a memorial in Tirunelveli district was laid in 2011, and on 20 August 2022, the anniversary of his death, India Post issued a commemorative stamp in his name."],
    ["n","The record of Ondiveeran rests heavily on tradition and on recent official citations rather than on contemporary documents. Some dates in popular accounts, such as a battle in 1775, cannot be right, since he died in 1771. We give only the dates that sources agree on."]
  ]
},

velu: {
  title: "Velu Nachiyar: the queen who came back",
  dek: "Widowed and driven from her kingdom in 1772, she spent eight years in exile building an alliance. In 1780 she marched home and took Sivaganga back from the Nawab and the Company.",
  body: [
    ["h","The princess of Ramanathapuram"],
    ["p","Velu Nachiyar was born on 3 January 1730 in Ramanathapuram, the only child of Raja Chellamuthu Vijayaragunatha Sethupathy and Rani Sakandhimuthal of the Sethupathi line. The king had no son, and she was raised as his heir. She was trained in arms, in valari, the curved Tamil throwing weapon, in silambam stick-fighting, in horse riding and in archery. The Sivaganga district administration records that she also knew French, English and Urdu."],
    ["p","At sixteen, in 1746, she married Muthu Vaduganatha Periya Udaiya Thevar, the second king of Sivaganga, a kingdom that had been carved out of the Ramanathapuram country only a few years before. They had one daughter, Vellachi."],
    ["h","Kalaiyarkoil, 1772"],
    ["p","Sivaganga refused the tribute the Nawab of Arcot demanded. In 1772 the Nawab's forces and Company troops, commanded according to the state text by Lieutenant Colonel Bon Jour, stormed the king's position at Kalaiyarkoil. Muthu Vaduganatha Thevar was killed. Contemporary summaries say the Nawab seized Sivaganga in June 1772."],
    ["p","Velu Nachiyar escaped with her small daughter and a few loyal men. Among them were two brothers who had served the king, later known as the Maruthu Pandiyar."],
    ["h","Eight years at Virupachi"],
    ["p","She found shelter far to the west, at Virupachi near Dindigul, under the palayakkarar Gopala Nayak. Dindigul was then Mysore territory, and Gopala Nayak needed Hyder Ali's leave to keep her. She stayed eight years."],
    ["p","They were years of preparation. Her minister, the Dalavay Thandavarayan Pillai, wrote to Hyder Ali on her behalf asking for 5,000 infantry and 5,000 cavalry, and set out her grievances against the Company. Hyder Ali, already at odds with the Nawab and the English, ordered his commandant Syed at Dindigul fort to give her the help she needed. In exile she raised and trained an army of her own, including a regiment of women."],
    ["h","1780: the march home"],
    ["p","In July 1780 Hyder Ali invaded the Carnatic, opening the Second Anglo-Mysore War. With the Nawab and the Company pinned down, Velu Nachiyar marched on Sivaganga, about a hundred kilometres away, with Mysore cavalry, Gopala Nayak's men and her own soldiers. The Maruthu brothers rode with her."],
    ["p","The decisive moment came at Sivaganga itself. Her agents had found where the Company kept its ammunition, and her commander Kuyili destroyed it. With the enemy's powder gone, the fort fell. The state text records that she defeated the Nawab's forces and recaptured Sivaganga, and that with the Maruthu brothers' help she was crowned queen again."],
    ["t","Popular accounts say her soldiers entered the fort disguised among the women coming for the Vijayadasami festival at the Rajarajeswari temple, and that Kuyili doused herself in oil and walked burning into the armoury. These details come from tradition and later retellings; the core fact, that the armoury was destroyed and the fort taken, is in the state record."],
    ["h","Queen of Sivaganga"],
    ["p","The Nawab, facing Hyder Ali and Velu Nachiyar together, came to terms: she would keep Sivaganga and pay him revenue. She made Periya Maruthu commander of her army and Chinna Maruthu her minister. When the English moved against Sivaganga again in 1783, the state text records that Maruthu Pandiyan saved the kingdom by negotiation."],
    ["p","Around 1790 she handed authority to her daughter Vellachi, while continuing to rule with the Maruthu brothers. The state text records that Vellachi died in 1790 in mysterious circumstances. Velu Nachiyar died on 25 December 1796 at Sivaganga."],
    ["h","Veeramangai"],
    ["p","Tamil memory calls her Veeramangai, the brave woman. She is widely recognised as the first Indian queen to wage war on the East India Company and win, seventy-seven years before the revolt of 1857. India Post issued a stamp in her honour on 31 December 2008, and the Tamil Nadu government opened the Veeramangai Velu Nachiyar Memorial at Sivaganga in 2014."],
    ["n","Main sources: Sivaganga District Administration; Tamil Nadu state history texts; Manonmaniam Sundaranar University; Live History India. Sources spell her husband's name in several ways; we follow the district record."]
  ]
},

kuyili: {
  title: "Kuyili: the fire in the armoury",
  dek: "Almost everything about her comes to us through folk song and memory. One act survives in every account: at Sivaganga in 1780, the Company's ammunition store went up in flames, and Kuyili died in it.",
  body: [
    ["h","A companion in exile"],
    ["p","Kuyili was a commander and close companion of Velu Nachiyar through the eight years of exile at Virupachi. Accounts collected on Wikipedia give her birth as 29 November 1749 at Kudanchavadi near Sivaganga and say she came from a poor and marginalised family. These biographical details come from oral history and later writing rather than from contemporary records."],
    ["t","Traditions remember that she saved the queen's life more than once, exposing a silambam teacher who was spying on the court and fighting off an attacker who came at the queen while she slept."],
    ["h","Sivaganga, 1780"],
    ["p","When Velu Nachiyar returned in 1780, the Company still held the fort at Sivaganga, and its guns and powder gave it the advantage. The queen's intelligence agents had found where the ammunition was stored. The Indian Culture portal of the Ministry of Culture records that Kuyili and the queen planned the attack on it together."],
    ["p","Kuyili reached the armoury and destroyed it, at the cost of her own life. The state history text says she doused herself with oil, set herself on fire and walked into the storehouse. Without its ammunition the garrison could not hold, and Sivaganga was retaken."],
    ["h","Udaiyaal"],
    ["p","Velu Nachiyar's women's regiment is remembered by the name Udaiyaal. The sources disagree about the name. One Tamil Nadu text says the unit was named after a shepherd girl killed for refusing to betray Kuyili's plans. Another says Udaiyaal was the queen's adopted daughter, who herself blew up a British arsenal. We record both."],
    ["h","Theepaanchi Amman"],
    ["p","In the village of Muthupatti near Sivaganga, local tradition reveres her as Theepaanchi Amman, the goddess who leapt into fire. She is often called the first woman martyr of India's freedom struggle and one of the first to give her life in this way. The Tamil Nadu government opened a memorial to her at Sivaganga in July 2014."],
    ["n","Historical evidence for Kuyili's life is scarce, and much of it comes from folk songs and oral history. The destruction of the armoury and its role in the fall of Sivaganga are recorded in state history texts and on the Ministry of Culture's portal."]
  ]
},

maruthu: {
  title: "The Maruthu brothers: rulers of Sivaganga, voices of 1801",
  dek: "Two brothers who rose from palace service to rule Sivaganga, sheltered the hunted, and in June 1801 nailed to the walls of Tiruchirappalli a call for every people of the south to rise together.",
  body: [
    ["h","Mukkulam"],
    ["p","Periya Maruthu, also called Vellai Maruthu, was born in 1748, and his younger brother Chinna Maruthu in 1753. Wikipedia names their birthplace as Mukkulam near Narikudi, about eighteen miles from Aruppukkottai; the India Post citation gives Periya Maruthu's birth date as 15 December 1748. Their father, Mookiah Palaniappan Servai, served Muthu Vaduganatha Thevar, the king of Sivaganga, and their mother was Anandhayee, also called Ponnathal."],
    ["p","As young men the brothers served in the palace and learned the arts of war. The king gave them the title Pandiyar."],
    ["t","Tradition says they won the king's favour by saving him from a tiger without weapons. Tradition also credits them with skill in making the valari, the Tamil throwing weapon."],
    ["h","Exile and return"],
    ["p","When the king was killed at Kalaiyarkoil in 1772, the brothers escaped with Velu Nachiyar to Virupachi. For eight years they trained and gathered men under Gopala Nayak's protection, and in 1780 they rode with the queen when she took back Sivaganga. She made Periya Maruthu commander of her army and Chinna Maruthu her chief minister."],
    ["p","Over the next two decades they became the real power in Sivaganga, first for the queen, then for her daughter Vellachi, and after both had died, in their own right. The state text records that between 1783 and 1801 they ran the kingdom's administration and public works. In 1783, when the English moved against Sivaganga again, it was Maruthu Pandiyan's diplomacy that saved it."],
    ["h","A league against the Company"],
    ["p","By the late 1790s the brothers were organising resistance across the south. The palayakkarars of Nagalapuram, Mannarkottai, Powalli, Kolarpatti and Chennulgudi formed a league through them. On 1 June 1799 Kattabomman came with 500 men to meet Maruthu at Palayanur and returned to Panchalankurichi with 500 more from Sivaganga."],
    ["p","After Kattabomman was hanged that October, the brothers took up his cause. When Oomaithurai and Sevathaiah broke out of Palayamkottai prison in February 1801, Chinna Maruthu took them to his capital, Siruvayal. When the British demanded the fugitives, the Maruthu brothers refused."],
    ["h","The proclamation"],
    ["p","On 16 June 1801 the brothers issued a proclamation of independence. It was posted on the walls of the Nawab's palace inside the Tiruchirappalli fort and at the Srirangam temple. It was addressed to the people of Jambudweepa, the old name for the Indian peninsula, and called on Hindus, Muslims and Christians, people of every caste, to unite and take up arms against the English."],
    ["p","In the proclamation's argument, the Europeans had taken the country by deceit, the people had grown poor under them, and rivalry among the people themselves had made it possible. It warned those who served the English and appealed to the priests and leading men of Srirangam for their blessing. A letter reporting the proclamation to the Company was found in the Tamil Nadu Government Archives in Chennai, and the Chief Secretary, J. Webbe, wrote about it to Lieutenant Colonel Agnew, who commanded in the south."],
    ["h","The war of 1801"],
    ["p","Chinna Maruthu gathered nearly 20,000 men. The Company brought reinforcements from Bengal, Ceylon and Malaya. The rajas of Pudukkottai, Ettayapuram and Thanjavur sided with the British. Forces from Sivaganga and Ramanathapuram, led by Chinna Maruthu's son Sevatha Thambi, marched up the coast towards Thanjavur, and distressed peasants joined them on the way."],
    ["p","Colonels Agnew and Innes marched on Sivaganga. The rebels fell back to Piranmalai and to the jungle stronghold around the Kalaiyarkoil temple. Accounts describe weeks of fighting in the forests, with heavy losses on both sides, before the stronghold fell in October 1801."],
    ["h","Tiruppattur"],
    ["p","The brothers were wounded and captured in battle at Cholapuram on 19 October 1801, according to the historian K. Rajayyan. On 24 October 1801 they were hanged at the fort of Tiruppattur, in present-day Sivaganga district."],
    ["p","They did not die alone. Their commanders, followers and male heirs were hanged with them, including boys of about ten and twelve. Rajayyan notes that the process was irregular, with no witness evidence taken under oath. Of Periya Maruthu's sons only Doraisamy was spared death, and he was banished to Penang with the other exiles."],
    ["p","A French priest of the time, quoted by Scroll, wrote that with Maruthu Pandiyan fell the last war of independence in southern India. Sivaganga was annexed, and the Carnatic Treaty that year ended the palayam system."],
    ["h","Memory"],
    ["p","The brothers' remains were buried at Kalaiyarkoil, where their memorial stands and where a guru pooja is held every year on 27 October. India Post issued a stamp on 24 October 2004. In 2007 a ten-foot bronze statue of the brothers on horseback was unveiled in Madurai."],
    ["n","Main sources: Sivaganga District Administration; Tamil Nadu state history texts; Scroll.in, drawing on K. Rajayyan's South Indian Rebellion: The First War of Independence 1800–1801; Swarajya on the archive letter. Some summaries wrongly give their execution date as 16 November 1801."]
  ]
},

katta: {
  title: "Veerapandiya Kattabomman: Panchalankurichi defiant",
  dek: "He followed a Collector four hundred miles to settle his tax, walked out of a trap at Ramanathapuram, and when the Company came with its guns, he would not bow. He was hanged at Kayathar at the age of thirty-nine.",
  body: [
    ["h","The forty-seventh lord of Panchalankurichi"],
    ["p","Veerapandiya Kattabomman was born on 3 January 1760 at Panchalankurichi, in present-day Thoothukudi district, to Jagaveera Kattabomman and Arumugathammal. Kattabomman Nayak was a family title; the family belonged to the Telugu-speaking Nayak palayakkarars of the eastern black-soil country. He became palayakkarar in 1792, at about thirty, on his father's death, the forty-seventh of his line."],
    ["p","His world was ruled by a debt. Under treaties of 1781 and 1792 the Nawab of Arcot had handed the Company the right to collect tribute from the palayams and to use force against those who fell behind. Company Collectors now dealt directly with the chieftains, and the state text records that they humiliated them and resorted to force."],
    ["h","Four hundred miles behind the Collector"],
    ["p","By 1798 Panchalankurichi's tribute was in arrears, 3,310 pagodas by the state text's figure, after a severe drought. The Collector of Ramnad, W. C. Jackson, wanted to send an army, but Madras refused, because its troops were committed against Tipu Sultan. On 18 August 1798 Jackson ordered Kattabomman to meet him at Ramanathapuram within two weeks."],
    ["p","Kattabomman tried to meet him on the way. Jackson was touring Courtallam, Chokkampatti, Sivagiri, Sattur and Srivilliputhur, and at each place refused to see him. Kattabomman followed him for twenty-three days and some four hundred miles, and reached Ramanathapuram on 19 September 1798."],
    ["h","The trap at Ramanathapuram"],
    ["p","Jackson received him that day. The accounts were checked and most of the arrears were found paid, with 1,080 pagodas outstanding. Even so, Kattabomman and his minister Sivasubramania Pillai were kept standing before the Collector, by the state text's account for three hours, and ordered to stay inside the fort. Sepoys appeared to arrest them."],
    ["p","Kattabomman broke out. His brother Oomaithurai forced his way into the fort with his men to help him. In the fight at the gate several men were killed, among them Lieutenant Clarke. Sivasubramania Pillai was captured; Kattabomman escaped."],
    ["t","Tamil tradition remembers Kattabomman asking why he should pay tax to the English, when it is the sky that gives the rain and the earth that gives the crop. The line is beloved and is central to the 1959 film, but it comes from tradition, not from the Company record."],
    ["h","A fair hearing"],
    ["p","Kattabomman did not go to war. He wrote to the Council at Madras and blamed Jackson. Governor Edward Clive appointed a committee of William Brown, William Oram and John Casamajor. On 15 December 1798 it found Kattabomman not guilty and condemned Jackson's conduct. Sivasubramania Pillai was released, Jackson was dismissed, and S. R. Lushington became Collector. Kattabomman paid off nearly all of what remained."],
    ["h","The road to war"],
    ["p","The peace did not hold. Lushington tried to stop Kattabomman from meeting the Maruthu brothers, who were drawing the palayams of the south into a league. Kattabomman joined it and became one of its leaders. In 1799 a column under his Dalavay Kumaraswami Nayak marched towards Sivagiri, a Company tributary at the foot of the Ghats, to win it over. Madras treated this as open rebellion."],
    ["p","In May 1799 Governor-General Lord Wellesley ordered troops from Tiruchirappalli, Thanjavur and Madurai into Tirunelveli, joined by Travancore's forces, under Major John Bannerman. On 1 September Bannerman ordered Kattabomman to meet him at Palayamkottai. Kattabomman refused to come without his armed followers."],
    ["h","The siege"],
    ["p","On 5 September 1799 Bannerman's army surrounded Panchalankurichi, a mud fort about 500 feet long and 300 feet wide. Cavalry under Lieutenant Dallas sealed it off. Captains O'Reilly and Bruce led the assault on the south gate while sepoy companies attacked the north face. The assault failed. One account in the journal record says four European officers were killed. Colonel James Welsh, who served in these campaigns, later praised the bravery of the defenders in his memoirs."],
    ["p","When European reinforcements arrived, the garrison slipped out of the fort by night. Accounts differ on whether this was around 7 September or as late as 16 September. Bannerman wrote to the neighbouring chieftains asking them to help hunt Kattabomman down."],
    ["h","Kolarpatti and Nagalapuram"],
    ["p","Dallas and O'Reilly caught up with Kattabomman's men at Kolarpatti. After a running fight his followers scattered and he escaped on horseback, but thirty-four of his principal people were taken, among them Sivasubramania Pillai. On 13 September 1799 Bannerman had Pillai hanged in the most public place in Nagalapuram and his head displayed on a pike at Panchalankurichi."],
    ["h","Betrayal in the Kalapur jungle"],
    ["p","Kattabomman went north into the Pudukkottai country. The British put a price on his head. Under pressure from the Company, the Raja of Pudukkottai, Vijaya Raghunatha Tondaiman, had his men search the jungles of Kalapur, and Kattabomman was taken and handed over. The state text also names the Raja of Ettayapuram among those who betrayed him. Accounts give the date of capture as 24 September or 1 October 1799."],
    ["h","Kayathar"],
    ["p","On 16 October 1799 Bannerman brought Kattabomman before an assembly of palayakkarars at Kayathar, near Tirunelveli. The state text says he admitted the charges against him. He was sentenced to death and hanged from a tamarind tree near the old fort of Kayathar. He was thirty-nine."],
    ["t","The citation issued with his 1999 stamp, based on material from a Kattabomman cultural association, says that he refused to be hanged by the agents of the Company, and took the rope hanging from the tamarind and put it round his own neck."],
    ["h","Erasing Panchalankurichi"],
    ["p","His relatives were sentenced to life imprisonment in Palayamkottai fort, among them Oomaithurai. The palayam was broken up and parts were given to Ettayapuram, Melamandai and Maniyachi. After the second war the Company razed the fort, had the site ploughed and sown with castor, and struck the name of Panchalankurichi from the district registers."],
    ["h","The name that was not erased"],
    ["p","It survived anyway, in ballads and kummi songs, and in the shrine that grew up at the place of his hanging. Tamil Nadu rebuilt the fort as a memorial in 1974. Sivaji Ganesan's film of 1959 made his story known across India. India Post issued a stamp on 16 October 1999, the two hundredth anniversary of his death, and the Indian Navy named its station at Vijayanarayanam INS Kattabomman."],
    ["n","Main sources: Tamil Nadu state history texts; Manonmaniam Sundaranar University; a journal study of the Poligar Rebellion of 1799–1801; India Post stamp citation; Wikipedia. One state text gives his birth year as 1761 and the hanging as 17 October; we follow the more common 16 October."]
  ]
},

sundar: {
  title: "Veeran Sundaralingam: the commander who blew up the powder",
  dek: "Kattabomman's commander is remembered for a single act during the siege of Panchalankurichi. The record of his life is slight; the memory of that act is not.",
  body: [
    ["h","Kottai Karungulam"],
    ["p","Sundaralingam was born at Kottai Karungulam in present-day Thoothukudi district. Wikipedia records his father as Pandiyan Kattana Karuppanan Kudumbanar, and his community as the Kudumbanar, an agricultural community of southern Tamil Nadu."],
    ["p","He served Veerapandiya Kattabomman as a commander in the war against the East India Company. Accounts credit him with the defence of the mud fort at Panchalankurichi and with the training of its soldiers."],
    ["h","The ammunition dump"],
    ["p","Most historical accounts say Sundaralingam was killed in 1799, during the First Palayakkarar War. The most widely held account is that he died blowing up a British ammunition dump during the siege of Panchalankurichi, robbing the besiegers of the powder for their guns."],
    ["t","Tradition tells that his companion Vadivu drove a herd of goats towards the dump at night while he hid among them under a woollen blanket. When a sentry raised the alarm, he seized the torch from her hand and leapt into the magazine, and she followed him."],
    ["h","Another view"],
    ["p","A second view, also recorded, places his death in the Second Palayakkarar War of 1800–1801, fighting beside Oomaithurai. The sources do not settle the question."],
    ["h","Memory and controversy"],
    ["p","In 1997 the Tamil Nadu government named a transport corporation after him, and the decision set off caste clashes in the south. In 2009 the government issued a policy note to build a memorial to him at Kavarnagiri."],
    ["n","Main source: Wikipedia, Veeran Sundaralingam, and a GKToday summary. No contemporary Company document naming him has been located for this edition."]
  ]
},

oomai: {
  title: "Oomaithurai: the silent brother",
  dek: "He could not speak, but he broke his brother out of a Collector's trap, broke himself out of a Company prison, and rebuilt a fort in six days. The second war of 1801 was his.",
  body: [
    ["h","Kumarasamy"],
    ["p","Oomaithurai's given name was Kumarasamy. He was the younger brother of Veerapandiya Kattabomman. He could not speak, and it was for this that the people called him Oomaithurai, the mute lord. Another brother, Sevathaiah, shared his fate."],
    ["p","He was at his brother's side from the start. At Ramanathapuram on 19 September 1798, when Collector Jackson's sepoys moved to arrest Kattabomman, the state text records that Oomaithurai forced his way into the fort with his men and helped his brother escape."],
    ["h","Prisoner at Palayamkottai"],
    ["p","After Kattabomman was hanged in October 1799, the Madras Council sentenced his relatives to perpetual imprisonment in the fort at Palayamkottai. Oomaithurai and Sevathaiah were among them. They spent more than a year there."],
    ["h","2 February 1801"],
    ["p","On 2 February 1801 they escaped. The Maruthu brothers had planned the break, and accounts describe men gathering outside the prison disguised as sellers of banana leaves and firewood. The brothers went first to Kamudhi, where Chinna Maruthu met them and took them to his capital, Siruvayal."],
    ["h","A fort in six days"],
    ["p","Then they went home. The fort at Panchalankurichi had been pulled down in 1799. Oomaithurai's followers rebuilt it in six days. When Colin Macaulay's force came up from Palayamkottai in February 1801, it found a fort standing again and thousands of armed men inside. Rebels harried the British flanks and attacked their camp at night; Macaulay withdrew to Palayamkottai."],
    ["p","Through February and March the rebels held the field. Captain Hazard was repulsed at Kadalkudi; Major Sheppard's force was routed near Srivaikuntam. At Pasuvanthanai rebels attacked the British camp and it lost ninety-six men. When Macaulay's heavy guns finally breached Panchalankurichi's wall, the assault was thrown back with heavy losses. The journal record counts it as the fifth British reverse before the fort."],
    ["h","The fall"],
    ["p","Lieutenant Colonel Agnew took command, assembled a larger army at Kovilpatti, and marched on the fort. In May 1801, after a long bombardment, Panchalankurichi fell; its defenders fought until almost all were dead. Oomaithurai escaped, wounded by some accounts, and made his way to the Maruthu brothers in the jungles around Kalaiyarkoil."],
    ["p","In the war that followed he led forces from Sivaganga and Virupachi and fought on through the summer and autumn. When Kalaiyarkoil fell in October 1801, he was taken."],
    ["h","16 November 1801"],
    ["p","On 16 November 1801 Oomaithurai and Sevathaiah were executed at Panchalankurichi, at the fort they had rebuilt. Afterwards the Company had the site ploughed up and sown with castor oil seed and salt, so that it would never again be inhabited."],
    ["n","Main sources: Tamil Nadu state history texts; a journal study of the Poligar Rebellion of 1799–1801; Wikipedia. Sources disagree on whether the brothers were hanged or beheaded; the state text says beheaded."]
  ]
},

gopala: {
  title: "Gopala Nayak of Virupachi: host of the resistance",
  dek: "An old chieftain in the Palani hills gave a fugitive queen eight years of shelter, and twenty years later called the leaders of the south together to plan a war.",
  body: [
    ["h","The palayakkarar of Virupachi"],
    ["p","Virupachi lies near Oddanchatram, in the hill country between Dindigul and Palani. In the second half of the eighteenth century its palayakkarar was Gopala Nayak, who, the Dindigul district record says, commanded the Dindigul division of the palayakkarars. Dindigul then belonged to Mysore, first under Hyder Ali and later Tipu Sultan, until Tipu ceded it to the English in 1792."],
    ["h","Sanctuary"],
    ["p","When Sivaganga fell in 1772, Velu Nachiyar, her daughter and the Maruthu brothers fled to him. The Dindigul district record says he let them stay in his fort with Hyder Ali's permission. They stayed eight years, and when the queen marched home in 1780 Gopala Nayak's men marched with her."],
    ["h","The Dindigul League"],
    ["p","After 1792 the Company ruled Dindigul directly, and Gopala Nayak turned against it. The state text records that he led a Dindigul League with Lakshmi Nayak of Manaparai and Poojai Nayak of Devadanapatti, and drew inspiration from Tipu Sultan, who sent a deputation to show support."],
    ["h","Virupachi, April 1800"],
    ["p","In April 1800 the leaders of a great confederacy met at Virupachi to plan a rising: Maruthu Pandiyan of Sivaganga, Gopala Nayak of Dindigul, Kerala Varma of Malabar, and Krishnappa Nayak and Dhoondaji of Mysore. A speech in the Lok Sabha by the Palani MP S. K. Kharventhan gives the date of the meeting in the Palani hills as 29 April 1800 and says it planned an attack on the British fort at Coimbatore for 3 June 1800."],
    ["p","The rising began in Coimbatore in June 1800 and spread to Ramanathapuram and Madurai. The palayakkarars of Coimbatore, Sathyamangalam and Tarapuram were captured and hanged. Gopala Nayak fought on from the Anamalai hills, where, the state text says, the local peasants supported him, and later joined Oomaithurai."],
    ["h","The end"],
    ["p","He was overpowered in 1801. The historian K. Rajayyan, cited by Scroll, notes that the seventy-three-year-old Gopala Nayak was among the leaders executed before the Madras Government had even confirmed the sentences."],
    ["n","Main sources: Dindigul District Administration; Tamil Nadu state history text; Lok Sabha debates; Scroll.in citing K. Rajayyan. The exact date and place of his execution are not given in the sources we consulted."]
  ]
},

chinna: {
  title: "Dheeran Chinnamalai: the lion of Kongu",
  dek: "The war in the far south ended in 1801. In the Kongu country it went on for four more years, until a chieftain who had fought beside Tipu Sultan was hanged on Sankagiri hill.",
  body: [
    ["h","Theerthagiri"],
    ["p","He was born Theerthagiri on 17 April 1756 at Melapalayam near Kangeyam, in present-day Tiruppur district, to Rathnasamy and Periyatha of the Mandradiar family. He was trained in silambam, archery, horse riding and the arts of war. The Kongu country was then ruled by Mysore."],
    ["h","How he got his name"],
    ["p","The state text tells how Tipu Sultan's Diwan, Mohammed Ali, was carrying the tax collected in Kongu back to Mysore. Theerthagiri stopped him and seized the money, telling him to report that it had been taken by Chinnamalai, the one who stands between Sivanmalai and Chennimalai. The Diwan sent a force against him, and they fought on the bed of the Noyyal river. Theerthagiri won, and he was known ever after as Dheeran Chinnamalai, Chinnamalai the brave."],
    ["h","With Tipu"],
    ["p","He nevertheless came to serve Tipu Sultan against the English. The India Post citation records that he raised a band of a thousand young Kongu fighters, and that they took part in Tipu's victories at Chitheswaram and Mazhavalli and in the defence of Srirangapatna. When Srirangapatna fell and Tipu was killed in 1799, Chinnamalai went home to Kongu."],
    ["h","Odanilai"],
    ["p","He built a fort at Odanilai, near Arachalur in present-day Erode district, and made it his base against the Company. In 1800 he joined the confederacy of the south and sought the help of the Maruthu brothers and of the Marathas for an attack on the British at Coimbatore. The plan failed."],
    ["p","He fought on regardless. The India Post citation and the state record list his victories over the Company: on the banks of the Cauvery in 1801, at Odanilai in 1802, and at Arachalur in 1804."],
    ["h","Karumalai"],
    ["p","At last the Company came against Odanilai with cannon. Chinnamalai left the fort rather than be destroyed in it and took to guerrilla war, operating from Karumalai in the Palani hills. There his cook told the British where he was."],
    ["p","Chinnamalai and his brothers were captured and taken to Sankagiri fort. They refused to accept British rule, and on 31 July 1805 they were hanged at the top of the fort. Some records give the date as 2 August. Tamil accounts name the cook as Nallappan."],
    ["h","The last of them"],
    ["p","Chinnamalai is often called the last of the great Palayakkarar fighters. The Tamil Nadu government built memorials to him at Odanilai and Sankagiri and named the Erode collectorate after him. India Post issued a stamp in his honour on 31 July 2005, the two hundredth anniversary of his death."],
    ["n","Main sources: Tamil Nadu state history text; India Post stamp citation; Wikipedia. Popular accounts add colourful details, such as the names of British commanders he defeated; we have left out those we could not confirm."]
  ]
},

/* ───────────── THE OTHER SIDE ───────────── */

yusuf: {
  title: "Yusuf Khan: the Company's commander who turned",
  dek: "Born Maruthanayagam Pillai, he took Puli Thevar's forts for the Company, then declared himself ruler of Madurai and was hanged by the Company he had served.",
  body: [
    ["p","Maruthanayagam Pillai was born in 1725 at Keelapanaiyur, in present-day Ramanathapuram district. He converted to Islam and took the name Muhammad Yusuf Khan, joined the Company's sepoys and was trained under Major Stringer Lawrence. He served at the siege of Tiruchirappalli and rose to command the Company's Indian troops."],
    ["p","In March 1756 he was sent to Madurai to collect revenue and restore order, and from 1759 he rented Madurai and Tirunelveli for a lakh of rupees a year. It was he who besieged Nerkattumseval for two months and took Puli Thevar's three great forts on 16 May 1761. The state text also credits him with encouraging Madurai's weavers and restoring temple lands."],
    ["p","His lease was not renewed, and he quarrelled with his masters when they ordered him to serve the Nawab of Arcot. He began to negotiate with the palayakkarars on his own and proclaimed himself ruler of Madurai and Tirunelveli. The Company besieged Madurai in 1763 and again in 1764."],
    ["p","He was betrayed by three of his own men, his Diwan Srinivasa Rao, the French officer Marchand and Baba Sahib, who had been bribed. He was seized during his morning prayers and hanged on 15 October 1764 near Sammattipuram on the Madurai–Dindigul road. His death opened the way for Puli Thevar's return."],
    ["n","Main sources: Tamil Nadu state history text; Wikipedia, Maruthanayagam Pillai."]
  ]
},
heron: {
  title: "Colonel Alexander Heron",
  dek: "The commander of the Company's first expedition into Tirunelveli.",
  body: [
    ["p","In March 1755 Heron marched into Madurai and Tirunelveli with Mahfuz Khan, the Nawab's elder brother, and a force of about 500 Europeans and 200 sepoys. Madurai fell easily. Urged to deal with Puli Thevar, he moved against Nerkattumseval but abandoned the attack because he had no cannon, no supplies and no money to pay his men. He retreated to Madurai, and the Company recalled and dismissed him."],
    ["n","Main sources: Tamil Nadu state history text; Manonmaniam Sundaranar University."]
  ]
},
jackson: {
  title: "W. C. Jackson, Collector of Ramnad",
  dek: "The Collector whose handling of Kattabomman turned an arrears dispute into a war.",
  body: [
    ["p","In 1798 Jackson demanded Panchalankurichi's arrears, toured the country refusing to meet Kattabomman, and finally received him at Ramanathapuram on 19 September 1798. He kept him standing and tried to have him arrested; in the clash at the gate Lieutenant Clarke was killed. A committee appointed by Governor Edward Clive found Kattabomman not guilty on 15 December 1798 and condemned Jackson's conduct. Jackson was dismissed and replaced by S. R. Lushington."],
    ["n","Main sources: Tamil Nadu state history texts."]
  ]
},
banner: {
  title: "Major John Bannerman",
  dek: "The officer who besieged Panchalankurichi and hanged Kattabomman.",
  body: [
    ["p","Bannerman commanded the Company's 1799 expedition into Tirunelveli with sweeping powers from Lord Wellesley. He laid siege to Panchalankurichi on 5 September 1799, had Sivasubramania Pillai hanged at Nagalapuram on 13 September, and on 16 October 1799 brought Kattabomman before an assembly of palayakkarars at Kayathar, where he was sentenced and hanged. The state text calls the proceeding a mock trial."],
    ["n","Main sources: Tamil Nadu state history texts; India Post stamp citation."]
  ]
},
agnew: {
  title: "Lieutenant Colonel P. A. Agnew",
  dek: "The commander who ended the war of 1801.",
  body: [
    ["p","Agnew took command after Colin Macaulay's attacks on the rebuilt Panchalankurichi failed. He gathered a larger army at Kovilpatti and took the fort in May 1801 after a long bombardment. With Colonel Innes he then marched on Sivaganga and broke the rebels' jungle stronghold at Kalaiyarkoil in October 1801. The Madras Council wrote to praise him on 15 October 1801. The Tamil Nadu archive letter about the Srirangam proclamation was addressed to him."],
    ["n","Main sources: Tamil Nadu state history texts; journal study of the Poligar Rebellion; Swarajya."]
  ]
},
tondai: {
  title: "Vijaya Raghunatha Tondaiman, Raja of Pudukkottai",
  dek: "The ruler whose men captured Kattabomman.",
  body: [
    ["p","Pudukkottai had sided with the English since Puli Thevar's time. When Kattabomman fled north after the fall of Panchalankurichi in 1799, the Raja, under pressure from the Company, had his men search the Kalapur jungles. Kattabomman was captured and handed to the British. In 1801 Pudukkottai again sided with the Company against the Maruthu brothers."],
    ["n","Main sources: Tamil Nadu state history texts; Wikipedia."]
  ]
},

/* ───────────── EVENTS ───────────── */

e1529: { title:"The making of the palayams", dek:"How the Madurai country came to be held by seventy-two military chieftains.", body:[
  ["p","After the decline of Vijayanagara, its viceroys in Madurai, Nagama Nayak and his son Viswanatha Nayak, became independent rulers of the Madurai and Tirunelveli country. On the advice of his minister Ariyanatha Mudaliar, Viswanatha Nayak divided the land into seventy-two palayams. The system is usually dated to around 1529."],
  ["p","Madurai was ringed with a fort of seventy-two bastions, and each was placed under one of the chiefs. A palayakkarar paid a fixed annual tribute or supplied troops, kept order in his territory, judged civil and criminal cases and kept part of the revenue. By one common division, a third of the tax went to the Nayak, a third to the army and a third to the palayakkarar."],
  ["p","The palayams fell into two broad groups: the western palayams of the Tirunelveli country, mostly held by Maravar chieftains, and the eastern palayams of the black-soil tracts, held by Telugu Nayak families who had settled there. Two centuries later, that difference would shape who joined which side."],
  ["n","Sources: Tamil Nadu state history text; GKToday."]
]},
e1755: { title:"The first expedition turns back", dek:"March 1755: the Company's first attempt to bring the western palayams to heel ends without a battle won.", body:[
  ["p","In March 1755 the Nawab of Arcot sent his elder brother Mahfuz Khan into Madurai and Tirunelveli with Company troops under Colonel Alexander Heron. Madurai fell easily. The next target was Puli Thevar of Nerkattumseval, who refused the Nawab's tribute and led the western palayakkarars."],
  ["p","Heron never took the fort. He had no cannon, no supplies, and no money to pay his soldiers, and he abandoned the plan and retreated to Madurai. The Company recalled and dismissed him. Puli Thevar was left in command of the field, and he used it to build a league against the Nawab."],
  ["n","Some popular accounts describe Heron's guns failing against the fort's walls. The state history text says the attack was abandoned for want of cannon. Sources: Tamil Nadu state history text; Manonmaniam Sundaranar University."]
]},
e1756: { title:"The league of the west, and Kalakkad", dek:"Puli Thevar unites the western palayams, wins over Travancore, and routs Mahfuz Khan's army.", body:[
  ["p","Puli Thevar drew the palayams of Uthumalai, Surandai, Thalaivankottai, Naduvakurichi, Singampatti, Urkad, Seithur, Kollamkondan and Wadakarai into a confederacy. He courted the Pathan officers who held Madurai and Tirunelveli for Chanda Sahib, and asked Hyder Ali and the French for help. To win Travancore, he promised to restore Kalakkad to its Raja."],
  ["p","Mahfuz Khan came against him with 1,000 Company sepoys, 600 of the Nawab's and Carnatic cavalry and infantry. Before he could post his troops near Kalakkad, 2,000 Travancore soldiers joined Puli Thevar, and Mahfuz Khan's army was routed. From 1756 to 1763 the Tirunelveli palayakkarars under Puli Thevar were in near-constant rebellion."],
  ["n","The state text gives no exact date for Kalakkad. Sources: Tamil Nadu state history texts; Manonmaniam Sundaranar University."]
]},
e1761: { title:"Three forts fall to Yusuf Khan", dek:"16 May 1761: after two months of bombardment, Nerkattumseval, Vasudevanallur and Panayur are taken.", body:[
  ["p","Yusuf Khan, the Company's commander of Indian troops, refused to attack Puli Thevar without heavy guns. The siege train from Tiruchirappalli arrived only in September 1760, held up by the Company's wars with the French, Hyder Ali and the Marathas. A first attempt on Vasudevanallur failed."],
  ["p","Then the guns began on Nerkattumseval, and the bombardment went on for about two months. On 16 May 1761 Puli Thevar's three main forts fell. The fall of Pondicherry cost him his French allies, and Travancore, Seithur, Uthumalai and Surandai changed sides. Puli Thevar went into hiding."],
  ["n","Sources: Tamil Nadu state history texts; Wikipedia, Maruthanayagam Pillai."]
]},
e1764: { title:"Yusuf Khan hanged; Puli Thevar returns", dek:"1764: the Company executes its own rebel commander, and the lord of Nerkattumseval comes home.", body:[
  ["p","Yusuf Khan fell out with the Company, dealt with the palayakkarars on his own and declared himself ruler of Madurai. After sieges in 1763 and 1764, three of his own men, bribed, handed him over. He was hanged near Madurai on 15 October 1764."],
  ["p","With Yusuf Khan gone, Puli Thevar came out of hiding and retook Nerkattumseval the same year, and resumed his resistance."],
  ["n","Sources: Tamil Nadu state history text; Wikipedia."]
]},
e1767: { title:"Captain Campbell takes Nerkattumseval", dek:"1767: the last siege, and the disappearance of Puli Thevar.", body:[
  ["p","The Company sent Captain Campbell against Nerkattumseval. He laid siege and captured the fort. Puli Thevar escaped once more, and with that he passes out of the historical record. The state history text says that nothing is definitely known of his last days, and that he died in exile."],
  ["t","Local tradition holds that he was taken and escaped from his guards at the Sankarankoil temple, leaving only broken chains. Some accounts give 16 October 1767 as the day he died."],
  ["n","Sources: Tamil Nadu state history texts."]
]},
e1771: { title:"Ondiveeran falls near Manur", dek:"20 August 1771: Puli Thevar's commander dies fighting the Company.", body:[
  ["p","Four years after Nerkattumseval fell for the last time, Ondiveeran was still fighting. The India Post citation says he had taken charge of Puli Thevar's children after their father's disappearance. On 20 August 1771, near Manur in Tirunelveli district, he died in battle with the Company's forces."],
  ["n","Sources: Wikipedia, Ondiveeran; India Post stamp citation, 2022."]
]},
e1772: { title:"Sivaganga is overrun", dek:"1772: the king is killed at Kalaiyarkoil, and the queen flees west.", body:[
  ["p","Sivaganga had refused the Nawab of Arcot's tribute. In 1772 the Nawab's forces and Company troops, under Lieutenant Colonel Bon Jour by the state text's account, stormed the royal camp at Kalaiyarkoil. Raja Muthu Vaduganatha Thevar was killed; the Sivaganga district record says by British soldiers and the son of the Nawab. The Nawab took Sivaganga in June 1772."],
  ["p","Velu Nachiyar escaped with her infant daughter Vellachi and a few loyal followers, among them the Maruthu brothers, and found refuge with Gopala Nayak at Virupachi near Dindigul."],
  ["n","Sources: Sivaganga District Administration; Tamil Nadu state history text; Live History India."]
]},
e1780: { title:"Velu Nachiyar takes back Sivaganga", dek:"1780: the exiled queen marches home with Mysore cavalry, and Kuyili destroys the Company's powder.", body:[
  ["p","After eight years of exile, and with help sought from Hyder Ali by her minister Thandavarayan Pillai, Velu Nachiyar was ready. In July 1780 Hyder Ali invaded the Carnatic, tying down the Nawab and the Company. The queen marched on Sivaganga with Mysore troops from Dindigul, Gopala Nayak's men, her own regiments and the Maruthu brothers."],
  ["p","Her agents had found where the Company stored its ammunition. Kuyili destroyed it, giving her own life. With its powder gone the garrison could not hold, and Sivaganga was retaken. Velu Nachiyar was crowned queen again, and the Nawab, facing her and Hyder Ali together, agreed to let her keep the kingdom in return for revenue."],
  ["t","Popular accounts say her soldiers entered the fort among the crowds at the Vijayadasami festival at the Rajarajeswari temple."],
  ["n","Sources: Sivaganga District Administration; Indian Culture portal; Tamil Nadu state history texts; Live History India."]
]},
e1792: { title:"The tribute is signed away", dek:"1781 and 1792: the Nawab gives the Company the right to tax the palayams directly.", body:[
  ["p","In 1781, during the war with Mysore, the Nawab of Arcot assigned the revenue of the Carnatic, including Tirunelveli, to the Company. The treaty of 1792 went further, giving the Company direct power to collect tribute from the palayakkarars and to use force against those who failed to pay."],
  ["p","From then on, the palayakkarars dealt not with the Nawab's agents but with Company Collectors, who, the state text records, humiliated them and resorted to force. The Company also took over the village watch, the desakaval, and raised its fees above custom. These grievances lay behind the wars of 1799 and 1801."],
  ["n","Sources: GKToday; Tamil Nadu state history texts; journal study of the Poligar Rebellion of 1799–1801."]
]},
e1798: { title:"The meeting at Ramanathapuram", dek:"19 September 1798: Kattabomman walks into the Collector's fort, and fights his way out.", body:[
  ["p","On 18 August 1798 the Collector, W. C. Jackson, ordered Kattabomman to meet him at Ramanathapuram over arrears of tribute. Kattabomman followed Jackson's tour for twenty-three days and four hundred miles before he was received, on 19 September 1798. The accounts showed most of the arrears paid, with 1,080 pagodas outstanding."],
  ["p","Even so, Kattabomman and his minister Sivasubramania Pillai were made to stand before the Collector and ordered to remain inside the fort. When sepoys moved to arrest them, Kattabomman broke free, helped by Oomaithurai, who forced his way in with his men. Lieutenant Clarke and others were killed at the gate. Pillai was captured; Kattabomman escaped."],
  ["p","Kattabomman appealed to Madras. On 15 December 1798 a committee found him not guilty. Jackson was dismissed, Pillai was freed and S. R. Lushington became Collector."],
  ["n","Sources: Tamil Nadu state history texts; Manonmaniam Sundaranar University."]
]},
e1799a: { title:"Bannerman besieges Panchalankurichi", dek:"5 September 1799: the First Palayakkarar War begins at the gates of a mud fort.", body:[
  ["p","In May 1799 Lord Wellesley ordered troops from Tiruchirappalli, Thanjavur and Madurai into Tirunelveli, joined by Travancore's forces, under Major John Bannerman. On 1 September Bannerman ordered Kattabomman to meet him at Palayamkottai; Kattabomman would not come without his armed men."],
  ["p","On 5 September 1799 the army reached Panchalankurichi, a mud fort about 500 feet by 300. Cavalry under Lieutenant Dallas surrounded it, and Captains O'Reilly and Bruce stormed the south gate while sepoys attacked from the north. The assault failed with heavy losses. When reinforcements came, the garrison slipped out by night and withdrew towards Kadalkudi."],
  ["n","Sources disagree on when the garrison left: about 7 September or 16 September. Sources: journal study of the Poligar Rebellion; Tamil Nadu state history texts; India Post citation."]
]},
e1799s: { title:"Sundaralingam and the powder store", dek:"1799: during the siege, Kattabomman's commander destroys a British ammunition dump.", body:[
  ["p","By most historical accounts, Veeran Sundaralingam died in 1799 during the First Palayakkarar War. The most widely held account is that he was killed blowing up a British ammunition dump during the siege of Panchalankurichi."],
  ["t","Tradition says he crept up to the dump hidden among a herd of goats driven by his companion Vadivu, and that both died in the explosion."],
  ["n","Another view places his death in 1800–1801. Source: Wikipedia, Veeran Sundaralingam."]
]},
e1799k: { title:"Kolarpatti, and the hanging at Nagalapuram", dek:"September 1799: Kattabomman's men are caught, and his minister is executed.", body:[
  ["p","After the garrison left Panchalankurichi, Lieutenant Dallas and Captain O'Reilly overtook Kattabomman's men at Kolarpatti. The followers scattered and Kattabomman escaped on horseback, but thirty-four of his principal people were captured, among them his minister Sivasubramania Pillai."],
  ["p","On 13 September 1799 Bannerman had Pillai hanged in the most public place in Nagalapuram, and his head was displayed on a pike at Panchalankurichi. Nagalapuram and the other strongholds were subdued, and the western palayakkarars surrendered."],
  ["n","Sources: journal study of the Poligar Rebellion; Manonmaniam Sundaranar University; Tamil Nadu state history text."]
]},
e1799c: { title:"Captured in the Kalapur jungle", dek:"Late September 1799: betrayed in the Pudukkottai country.", body:[
  ["p","Kattabomman fled north into the Pudukkottai country with a price on his head. Under pressure from the Company, Raja Vijaya Raghunatha Tondaiman had the jungles of Kalapur searched, and Kattabomman was captured and handed to the British. The state text names the Raja of Ettayapuram too among those who betrayed him."],
  ["n","Sources give the date of capture as 24 September or 1 October 1799. Sources: Tamil Nadu state history texts; Wikipedia."]
]},
e1799h: { title:"Kattabomman is hanged at Kayathar", dek:"16 October 1799: a trial before the palayakkarars, and a tamarind tree.", body:[
  ["p","Bannerman brought Kattabomman before an assembly of palayakkarars at Kayathar, near Tirunelveli, so that they would see what became of those who resisted. The state text says Kattabomman admitted the charges. He was sentenced to death and hanged from a tamarind tree near the old fort. He was thirty-nine."],
  ["p","His family were imprisoned for life at Palayamkottai. His palayam was broken up and handed out to its neighbours."],
  ["t","The citation for his 1999 stamp says he would not let the Company's men hang him, and put the noose round his own neck."],
  ["n","One state text gives the date of hanging as 17 October. Sources: Tamil Nadu state history texts; India Post citation."]
]},
e1800a: { title:"The confederacy meets at Virupachi", dek:"April 1800: the leaders of the south plan a single war.", body:[
  ["p","In April 1800 the leaders of a confederacy met at Virupachi, Gopala Nayak's seat near Dindigul. With him were Maruthu Pandiyan of Sivaganga, Kerala Varma of Malabar, and Krishnappa Nayak and Dhoondaji of Mysore. It was the widest alliance yet against the Company, reaching from the Tirunelveli coast to the hills of Malabar."],
  ["p","A Lok Sabha speech records the meeting in the Palani hills on 29 April 1800, with leaders arriving from as far as Maharashtra, and says it planned an attack on the British fort at Coimbatore for 3 June 1800."],
  ["n","Sources: Tamil Nadu state history texts; Lok Sabha debates."]
]},
e1800b: { title:"The rising at Coimbatore", dek:"June 1800: the second war begins in the west.", body:[
  ["p","The uprising broke out in Coimbatore in June 1800 and spread to Ramanathapuram and Madurai. The Company declared war on Krishnappa Nayak, Kerala Varma and the others. The palayakkarars of Coimbatore, Sathyamangalam and Tarapuram were captured and hanged."],
  ["p","Dheeran Chinnamalai had sought the help of the Maruthu brothers and the Marathas for the attack on Coimbatore. It failed, and he withdrew to the Kongu country to fight on alone."],
  ["n","Sources: Tamil Nadu state history texts; Manonmaniam Sundaranar University."]
]},
e1801a: { title:"The escape from Palayamkottai", dek:"2 February 1801: Oomaithurai and Sevathaiah break out.", body:[
  ["p","Kattabomman's brothers had been held in the fort at Palayamkottai since 1799. On 2 February 1801 they escaped. The break had been planned with the Maruthu brothers, and accounts describe men gathering outside disguised as sellers of banana leaves and firewood."],
  ["p","The brothers went to Kamudhi, where Chinna Maruthu took them to his capital, Siruvayal. Within days they were back at Panchalankurichi. The Second Palayakkarar War in the south had begun."],
  ["n","Sources: Tamil Nadu state history texts; Wikipedia, Oomaithurai."]
]},
e1801r: { title:"A fort rebuilt in six days", dek:"February–March 1801: the rebels hold the field.", body:[
  ["p","Oomaithurai's followers rebuilt the ruined fort at Panchalankurichi in six days. When Colin Macaulay's force arrived, it found the walls up again and thousands of armed men inside. Rebels attacked his camp from all sides at night and threatened his flanks, and he retreated to Palayamkottai."],
  ["p","Captain Hazard was repulsed at Kadalkudi, and Major Sheppard's force was routed near Srivaikuntam. At Pasuvanthanai the British camp lost ninety-six men. When Macaulay's guns finally breached the wall, the assault was thrown back. The journal record counts this as the fifth British reverse before Panchalankurichi."],
  ["n","Source: journal study of the Poligar Rebellion of 1799–1801."]
]},
e1801f: { title:"Panchalankurichi falls again", dek:"May 1801: Agnew takes the fort after a long bombardment.", body:[
  ["p","Lieutenant Colonel Agnew took command, gathered a larger army at Kovilpatti and marched on the fort. After a prolonged bombardment it fell in May 1801. Its defenders fought until almost all were dead. The rebels abandoned their other strongholds at Tuticorin and Kadalkudi."],
  ["p","Oomaithurai escaped and joined the Maruthu brothers in the jungles around Kalaiyarkoil."],
  ["n","One state text dates the recapture to April 1801 under Macaulay. Sources: Wikipedia; Tamil Nadu state history texts; journal study of the Poligar Rebellion."]
]},
e1801p: { title:"The Tiruchirappalli Proclamation", dek:"16 June 1801: a call to every people of the peninsula.", body:[
  ["p","On 16 June 1801 the Maruthu brothers issued a proclamation of independence. Copies were posted on the walls of the Nawab's palace in Tiruchirappalli fort and at the Srirangam temple. It addressed the people of Jambudweepa, Hindus, Muslims and Christians, of every caste, and called on them to unite against the English."],
  ["p","It argued that the Europeans had taken the country by deceit and reduced its people to poverty, and that rivalry among the people had allowed it. It warned those who served the English and asked the priests and leading men of Srirangam for their blessing."],
  ["p","The Company had it taken down. A letter reporting it was later found in the Tamil Nadu Government Archives; the Chief Secretary, J. Webbe, wrote about it to Lieutenant Colonel Agnew. Historians see in it one of the earliest calls in India for a common resistance to foreign rule."],
  ["n","Sources: Scroll.in; Swarajya; Tamil Nadu state history texts."]
]},
e1801t: { title:"The Carnatic Treaty", dek:"July 1801: the Nawab cedes the Carnatic, and the palayams end.", body:[
  ["p","While the war still raged in Sivaganga, the Nawab of Arcot signed away his territories. Under the Carnatic Treaty of July 1801 he ceded North and South Arcot, Tiruchirappalli, Madurai and Tirunelveli to the Company, with all powers of government."],
  ["p","The palayakkarar system, which had lasted more than two and a half centuries, was ended. Forts were demolished and armies disbanded. The palayakkarars who remained became zamindars, landholders without military power, and the Company enforced strict disarmament."],
  ["n","Sources give the date as 26 July or 31 July 1801. Sources: Wikipedia, Carnatic Treaty; Tamil Nadu state history texts; Swarajya."]
]},
e1801k: { title:"The fall of Kalaiyarkoil", dek:"October 1801: the jungle stronghold breaks, and the brothers are taken at Cholapuram.", body:[
  ["p","Through the summer the rebels fought from the forests around Piranmalai and the great temple of Kalaiyarkoil. Chinna Maruthu had gathered nearly 20,000 men; the Company brought reinforcements from Bengal, Ceylon and Malaya, and the rajas of Pudukkottai, Ettayapuram and Thanjavur fought on its side."],
  ["p","Colonels Agnew and Innes pressed into the jungle, and accounts describe weeks of fighting with heavy losses on both sides. Kalaiyarkoil fell in October 1801. On 19 October the Maruthu brothers, wounded, were captured in battle at Cholapuram."],
  ["n","Sources: Scroll.in, citing K. Rajayyan; Tamil Nadu state history texts; Wikipedia."]
]},
e1801m: { title:"The Maruthu brothers are hanged", dek:"24 October 1801: Tiruppattur fort.", body:[
  ["p","On 24 October 1801 Periya Maruthu and Chinna Maruthu were hanged at the fort of Tiruppattur, in present-day Sivaganga district. With them died their commanders, followers and male heirs, including boys of about ten and twelve."],
  ["p","The historian K. Rajayyan notes that no witness evidence was taken under oath. Only Periya Maruthu's son Doraisamy was spared death; he was exiled. The brothers were buried at Kalaiyarkoil, where their memorial now stands."],
  ["n","Sources: Sivaganga District Administration; Scroll.in; The Hindu via TNPSC Thervupettagam."]
]},
e1801o: { title:"Oomaithurai's end, and the exile to Penang", dek:"16 November 1801: the last of Kattabomman's brothers dies at Panchalankurichi.", body:[
  ["p","Oomaithurai and Sevathaiah, captured after the fall of Kalaiyarkoil, were executed at Panchalankurichi on 16 November 1801. The Company had the site of the fort ploughed and sown with castor and salt, and struck its name from the district registers."],
  ["p","Seventy-three rebel leaders were deported to Penang, then called Prince of Wales Island, in Malaya. The Second Palayakkarar War in the south was over."],
  ["n","Sources: Tamil Nadu state history texts; GKToday; Wikipedia."]
]},
e1805: { title:"Epilogue: Dheeran Chinnamalai is hanged", dek:"31 July 1805: Sankagiri.", body:[
  ["p","In the Kongu country the war went on. Dheeran Chinnamalai defeated the Company on the Cauvery in 1801, at Odanilai in 1802 and at Arachalur in 1804. When the British came with cannon, he left his fort and fought from Karumalai in the Palani hills."],
  ["p","Betrayed by his cook, he was captured with his brothers and taken to Sankagiri fort. They refused to accept British rule and were hanged at the top of the fort on 31 July 1805. With them ended the last great war of the palayakkarars."],
  ["n","Some records give 2 August 1805. Sources: Tamil Nadu state history text; India Post citation; Wikipedia."]
]}
};
